.he "select (2)                    10/28/26                    select (2)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
select
- wait for input on a set of file descriptors
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
.bd
select(mask)
.br
.bd
unsigned int mask;
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Select
waits until at least one of the descriptors named in
mask can be read, and returns the ones that can.
Bit n of mask is descriptor n; there are 16 descriptors,
so one word is the whole set.  Bits for descriptors that
are not open are ignored.

A terminal descriptor is readable when a read would not
block: a byte in the raw queue in raw or cbreak mode,
a line in the cooked queue in cooked mode, or, in any
mode, a hangup, in which case the read returns end of
file.  A descriptor on /dev/net is readable when the
network card holds a frame; the card keeps the frame
until the driver reads it, so select looks without
consuming it.  No other device is waited on: a regular
file, a pipe, or a device with no queue of its own is
not selectable.

There is no timeout and no set of descriptors to write.
This is not the BSD select.  A program that wants a
deadline sets an alarm: install a handler for the alarm
signal, arm
.bd
alarm
(see alarm (2)), and treat a -1 return as "nothing
yet".  The handler must re-arm itself, since this
system resets a caught signal to its default when the
signal is delivered.

.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
The subset of mask that can be read, in the same bit
numbering, or -1 if a signal arrived first.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ERRORS
.in +5
.br
.bd
EINTR
if a caught signal arrived before any descriptor became
readable.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ASSEMBLER
.in +5
.br
.bd
(select = 58)
.br
.bd
(mask in hl)
.br
.bd
sys; select
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
The readable subset of the mask is returned in hl.
The carry flag is cleared on success, or set on failure;
the error number is then in hl.
